{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"01","chapter_title":"Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming","title":"Resume Claims and Evidence","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting resume claims and evidence to resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming","cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/08"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_calibration"]},"content":"# Resume Claims and Evidence\n\n![Resume Claims and Evidence textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-01-resume-claims-and-evidence.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nEvery line on a resume is a claim about you, and a recruiter reads it as a promise you will have to keep in an interview. The line \"Managed social media\" claims you ran something; \"Improved sales\" claims you caused a result. Most first-draft resumes are full of claims with no evidence attached, so the reader cannot tell a real accomplishment from a hopeful phrase. This section teaches the move that separates a strong resume from a generic one: attaching a verifiable fact (a number, a scope, a named tool or outcome) to every claim you make.\n\nThe skill is concrete because recruiters scan fast. Studies of resume review put the first pass at roughly six to eight seconds, so a claim that cannot be checked at a glance is wasted space. Your deliverable, a before-and-after revision of one section plus an AI-use note, is the place where you prove you can turn a duty list into evidence and keep yourself honest while doing it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nMaria has a line on her resume that reads \"Responsible for inventory.\" It is true, but it is also what every stockroom worker could write. When a recruiter asked about it in a mock interview, Maria suddenly remembered the details: she had cut a recurring count error by reconciling the spreadsheet against the delivery logs every Friday, and the store stopped running out of two best-selling items. None of that was on the page. The accomplishment existed; the evidence for it was missing.\n\nThat gap is the heart of this section. The facts that make you hireable usually already exist in your memory, your pay stubs, your old schedules, or your project files. The work is not inventing impressive-sounding phrases; it is excavating the real evidence and writing it down so the reader does not have to interview you to find it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA resume bullet has two parts that students routinely collapse into one: the **claim** (what you say you did) and the **evidence** (the checkable fact that backs it). A claim without evidence is a duty; a claim with evidence is an accomplishment. The reliable pattern for an accomplishment bullet is **action verb plus scope plus result**: a verb that shows judgment (trained, reconciled, redesigned, not \"helped\" or \"responsible for\"), a scope that bounds it (how many, how often, how long), and a result the reader can picture.\n\nThree quality tests sharpen each bullet:\n\n| Test | Question it asks | Failure looks like |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Verifiability | Could you defend this fact if asked? | \"Boosted sales 30%\" with no source |\n| Specificity | Does a number or named scope appear? | \"Helped many customers\" |\n| Ownership | Did you do this, or just stand near it? | \"Was part of a team that...\" |\n\nNotice what these tests rule out. They reject inflation (claims you cannot defend), and they reject vagueness (claims too soft to check). Polish, fixing fonts, tightening spacing, adding strong adjectives, does not pass any of these tests, which is why a beautiful resume can still be a weak one. The evidence, not the styling, is what survives a recruiter's question.\n\n## Worked Example: Turning One Bullet Into Evidence\n\nWork through one real resume bullet, step by step, so you can see what \"evidence\" means in practice.\n\n**Step 1. Start with the weak bullet the student first wrote.**\n\n```\nCashier at Smoothie King. Helped customers and kept the store clean.\n```\n\nThis is a duty list. Any cashier could write it. There is no action verb that shows judgment, no number, and no scope.\n\n**Step 2. Pull the facts the student can actually defend.** From their pay stubs and a quick memory pass:\n\n| Fact | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Shifts per week | 4 |\n| Length of employment | 14 months |\n| Trained new hires | 5 |\n| Drive-thru order accuracy goal | 95% |\n\n**Step 3. Rewrite the bullet so each phrase maps to a fact.**\n\n```\nTrained 5 new cashiers over 14 months and held drive-thru order accuracy\nat or above the store's 95% goal across 4 shifts per week.\n```\n\n**Step 4. Audit the rewrite.** Every clause now points to a row in the table: \"trained 5\" maps to trained-new-hires, \"14 months\" to length, \"95% goal\" to accuracy, \"4 shifts per week\" to shifts. A recruiter can ask about any phrase and the student has a truthful answer. That traceability, not the polish, is what makes the bullet count as evidence.\n\n**Step 5. Record the AI boundary.** If an AI tool had suggested \"Boosted store revenue 30%,\" the student would reject it, because no row in the table supports that number, and log the rejection in the AI-use note.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Walk the bullet from duty to evidence\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You start with \\\"Cashier at Smoothie King. Helped customers and kept the store clean.\\\" Why does a recruiter learn almost nothing from it?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is too short to fit the page\",\"It has no judgment verb, no number, and no scope, so any cashier could write it\",\"It uses the wrong font\",\"It names the employer\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Right. \\\"Helped\\\" and \\\"kept clean\\\" describe presence, not judgment. With no count and no scope, the line is a duty list anyone could copy.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Before rewriting, what is the FIRST thing to gather?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A stronger adjective like \\\"passionately\\\"\",\"Defensible facts from pay stubs and memory (shifts, tenure, trainees, accuracy goal)\",\"A new template\",\"The recruiter’s name\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Evidence-first revision is sourcing, not writing. You pull facts you can defend: 4 shifts/week, 14 months, 5 trained, 95% accuracy goal.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Now each phrase maps to a fact: \\\"Trained 5 new cashiers over 14 months and held drive-thru order accuracy at or above the store’s 95% goal across 4 shifts per week.\\\"\"},{\"prompt\":\"An AI suggests adding \\\"Boosted store revenue 30%.\\\" What do you do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Accept it, it sounds impressive\",\"Reject it and log the rejection, because no row in the fact table supports 30%\",\"Round it to 25% to be safe\",\"Move it to the top\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Reject and log. Defensibility, not how good it sounds, is the standard. No source row supports 30%, so it cannot survive a recruiter’s question.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor each bullet, run it through the claim-evidence test in order: name the claim, then ask whether a checkable fact supports it. Keep the bullet only if you can answer yes truthfully.\n\n1. **Claim check:** What exactly am I saying I did? Underline the verb; if it is \"helped,\" \"responsible for,\" or \"involved in,\" it probably hides a stronger action.\n2. **Evidence check:** Which number, scope, tool, or outcome proves it? Point to the pay stub, schedule, project file, or supervisor who could confirm it.\n3. **Defense check:** If a recruiter asked \"tell me more about this,\" do I have a truthful, specific answer ready?\n\nIf a bullet fails the evidence check, your choices are to dig up the missing fact or to delete the bullet, never to invent the fact. A shorter resume of defensible claims beats a longer one of empty ones.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Claim check: underline the verb and name exactly what you say you did\",\"Evidence check: point to the number, scope, tool, or outcome that proves it\",\"Defense check: rehearse the truthful, specific answer if a recruiter asks more\",\"Decide: keep the bullet only if all three pass, else find the fact or cut it\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A draft reads \\\"passionately served many customers daily.\\\" Which test does it fail hardest?\" explanation=\"\\\"Passionately\\\" and \\\"many\\\" are an intensifier plus a vague quantity. There is no checkable number or named scope, so the bullet fails specificity (and verifiability). An adjective adds heat, not evidence.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Specificity, because no count or named scope appears\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Ownership, because someone else served the customers\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It passes all three tests\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Formatting, because the font is wrong\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating a stronger adjective as evidence. Changing \"helped customers\" to \"passionately served customers\" adds heat, not light; the reader still cannot check anything. Evidence is a fact (a count, a percentage against a stated goal, a named system), not an intensifier.\n\nA second misreading runs the other way: assuming honesty means modesty. Refusing to state a real achievement because it \"sounds like bragging\" buries evidence you are entitled to claim. The standard is not quiet, it is truthful and specific.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the pattern to a single bullet from a campus job. Suppose the draft reads \"Worked the front desk at the rec center.\" Start with the claim check: the verb \"worked\" describes presence, not judgment, so the bullet currently claims almost nothing. Move to the evidence: ask what you actually did and what is countable. You checked in members, you handled the equipment-rental log, and on busy nights the desk processed around 120 check-ins in a three-hour shift. Now rebuild with action plus scope plus result: \"Processed about 120 member check-ins per evening shift and maintained the equipment-rental log with zero unreturned items over the semester.\"\n\nRun the tests. Verifiability: the check-in count comes from the front-desk software, and the zero-loss record is on the rental sheet. Specificity: a number and a time window appear. Ownership: you, not \"the team,\" kept the log. The bullet went from a presence statement to an accomplishment without a single invented fact, because the facts were already in the rec center's own records. That is the whole move, repeated section by section.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this section does not just nod at \"use evidence.\" They start hunting their own paper trail before writing anything: old schedules for hours and shift counts, pay stubs for tenure, project folders for deliverables, performance notes for results. Evidence-first revision feels less like writing and more like sourcing.\n\nA strong reader can also tell the difference between the three failure modes and name which one a given bullet has: is it vague (no number), inflated (a number you cannot defend), or borrowed (a team result claimed as solely yours)? Diagnosing the specific failure is what tells you the specific fix.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same claim-evidence discipline moves straight into other documents in this module and beyond it. A LinkedIn \"About\" section, a scholarship application, a grad-school statement, and a performance self-review all reward the action-scope-result pattern, and all punish unverifiable claims. The constraint shifts with the audience: a scholarship committee may care about service hours and impact, while a tech recruiter cares about tools and measurable outcomes, so the same true facts get foregrounded differently. If you can re-aim which evidence leads without inventing new facts, you have understood the section rather than memorized one bullet.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-01-resume-claims-and-evidence-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note for CAT 100 02.01. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence","cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"],"stem":"A resume bullet reads: \"Helped run the campus food pantry.\" Which rewrite turns the claim into evidence a recruiter can verify?","options":["\"Passionate, hard-working volunteer dedicated to helping others.\"","\"Coordinated weekly intake for the campus food pantry, scheduling 12 volunteers and serving about 80 students per week across two semesters.\"","\"Helped out a lot at the campus food pantry and learned so much.\"","\"Food pantry experience (see references for details).\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verifiable claim names a concrete action, a number, and a scope. \"Coordinated weekly intake, 12 volunteers, 80 students, two semesters\" lets a reader check the scale and the role, unlike adjective-only or vague phrasing.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_adjectives_as_evidence","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You worked on a class team that built a website, and you wrote the page copy. Which bullet states a defensible claim without overstating your role?","options":["\"Built and launched a five-page marketing website that increased traffic 300%.\"","\"Single-handedly created the organization's entire web presence.\"","\"Wrote and edited the copy for a five-page team website that launched on time for the spring campaign.\"","\"Did website stuff for a class team project.\""],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Claiming credit for the whole site or a 300% gain you cannot trace is overstatement. Naming exactly your contribution (copy for five pages, launched on time) is both truthful and specific.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_inflating_scope","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence"],"stem":"An AI tool suggests adding \"Increased department sales by 25% through strategic initiatives\" to your retail resume, but you only stocked shelves and ran a register. What is the right move?","options":["Keep it; the AI knows what recruiters want to see.","Keep it but lower the number to 10% so it sounds more believable.","Reject it, replace it with a truthful bullet you can defend, and note in your AI-use log why you rejected it.","Keep it and add \"as part of the team\" so it is technically true."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An AI suggestion is a proposal, not a fact. A claim you cannot defend in an interview is fabrication even if the percentage is softened. Reject it, write a truthful bullet, and record the rejection.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_ai_invents_metrics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_resume_claims_and_evidence_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat100_supplement_resume_claims_and_evidence"],"stem":"Which before-after pair is the stronger revision because it moves from a duty to an action plus a measurable result?","options":["Before: \"Responsible for social media.\" After: \"Was in charge of all social media for the company.\"","Before: \"Responsible for social media.\" After: \"Scheduled and posted 4 Instagram posts per week, growing the account from 210 to 540 followers over a 10-week internship.\"","Before: \"Responsible for social media.\" After: \"Handled social media duties as assigned by my supervisor.\"","Before: \"Responsible for social media.\" After: \"Did social media and other marketing tasks.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong revision moves from a vague responsibility to a specific action plus a measurable outcome (4 posts/week, 210 to 540 followers, 10 weeks). The other options just restate the duty in different words.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_1_reword_not_revise","difficulty":"medium"}]}}